<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.9.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00212</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.9.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2914145a-8df9-4c28-be5b-0d246c2d50b3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice">
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <para>For numbers 1 – 9, use the following scenario: A car has a 16-gallon fuel tank. When
        driven on a highway, it has a gas mileage of 30 miles per gallon. The gas mileage (also
        called "fuel efficiency") tells us the number of miles the car can travel for a particular
        amount of fuel (one gallon of gasoline, in this case). After filling the gas tank, the
        driver got on a highway and drove for a while.</para>
     
        <exercise id="c9c0b944-2eb3-43b2-acc4-79148148453b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>How many miles has the car traveled if it has 15 gallons left in the tank?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 30 miles</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.9.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 30 miles.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_0b8af3c9-6742-4109-9f34-7f7fab8f173e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>How many miles has the car traveled if it has 10 gallons left in the tank?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 180 miles</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.9.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 180 miles.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_2ab2e5e8-03f0-40d4-898e-c6155a3fe6c4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>How many miles has the car traveled if it has 2.5 gallons left in the tank?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 405 miles</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.9.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 405 miles.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_97d672df-68be-43e5-8182-3eeb958b2ccd" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Choose the equation that represents the relationship between the distance the car
              has traveled in miles, \(d\), and the amount of gas left in the tank in gallons, \(x\)</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(d=16(30-x)\)</para>
            <para>\(x=\frac{d-480}{-30}\)</para>
            <para>\(d=30(16-x)\)</para>
            <para>\(x=\frac{30}{d-480}\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(d=30(16-x)\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.9.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(d=30(16-x)\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_6b70c89b-27d4-46db-830d-6009497c889c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>How many gallons are left in the tank when the car has traveled 90 miles?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 13 gallons</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.9.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 13 gallons.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_0c673f94-e584-4e9e-a231-7697c0dba0dc" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>How many gallons are left in the tank when the car has traveled 246 miles?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 7.8 gallons</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.9.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 7.8 gallons.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_3480b5d0-91da-4121-bd45-5ab49f90db3c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Choose an equation that makes it easier to find the amount of gas left in the
              tank, \(x\), if we know the car has traveled \(d\) miles.</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(d=16(30-x)\)</para>
            <para>\(x=\frac{d-480}{-30}\)</para>
            <para>\(d=30(16-x)\)</para>
            <para>\(x=\frac{30}{d-480}\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(x=\frac{d-480}{-30}\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.9.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(x=\frac{d-480}{-30}\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="c4617740-0dff-4561-ab05-fbbbcf1b0df6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Noah was helping to collect the entry fees at his school's football game. Student
              entry costs $2.75 each and adult entry costs $5.25 each. By the end of the game, Diego
              had collected $281.25.</para>
              <para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> equations that could represent the relationship between the number of
                students, \(s\), the number of adults, \(a\), and the dollar amount collected at the
                game.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \( 281.25 - 5.25a=2.75s \), \(a=53.57- \frac{2.75}{5.25}s \)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.9.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answers are \( 281.25 - 5.25a=2.75s \) and \(a=53.57-
              \frac{2.75}{5.25}s \)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
